title: A sealed envelope
subtitle: Zetkin has heard of papers that Moscow would like to keep shut.
new-page: true
tags: kpd_event
view-if: year = 1923 and month = 4 and test_stage = 0 and civil_war = 0

= A letter from Clara Zetkin

In April a letter reaches the Zentrale by a route that avoids the Comintern's post. It is from Clara Zetkin, who has known the Russian leaders since before the war and who is shown, to her own discomfort, a great deal that she is not supposed to tell us. Lenin has not spoken since his third stroke in March. In the last weeks before it he dictated notes to his secretaries about the future of the Russian party. They were sealed, and they lie with his wife, Nadezhda Krupskaya, who is to give them to a congress of the party after his death. In the Politburo they are known only to a few, and some of the few have an interest in keeping them in the envelope.

Zetkin writes that the notes are about the leaders, and that one of them is not spared. She does not say who. She says only that an old communist who believes the party's fate should not be settled by a secretary's office might think the German party the right place for a copy to be read, and she asks us to decide if we want one.

It is an unusual thing, a foreign party being asked to hold a secret of the Russian one. It would make us a party to the fight over Lenin's succession, which we have so far watched from the other side of a frontier. It would also be a form of trust: nobody offers such a paper to a party that cannot be relied on to keep silent.

- @ask: Ask Zetkin to send a courier.
- @decline: Decline. The Russian party's quarrels are not ours.

@decline
subtitle: Trust +3, Centre +2 strength. The chain ends here. · dev: test_stage = 9, trust +3, kf_centre_strength +2
on-arrival: test_stage = 9; comintern_trust += 3; kf_centre_strength += 2
go-to: root

We write back that the German party asks nothing of the Russian party that the Russian party does not wish to tell it. Zetkin accepts the answer with a short note that is not unfriendly. The Centre, which wants nothing to do with the fight in Moscow, is relieved. The envelope stays with Krupskaya, and whatever it says will be read, if it is read, by others.

@ask
title: Lenin's last notes
subtitle: A copy has arrived. · dev: test_stage = 1
on-arrival: test_stage = 1

= What the notes say

The courier comes at night with a small packet. It contains copies, in a secretary's hand, of what Lenin dictated in the last weeks of December 1922 and the first days of January 1923. We read them in the Zentrale with the shutters closed.

The first part is a letter to the next party congress. Lenin proposes to enlarge the Central Committee from its present number to fifty or a hundred, so that the party is no longer governed by a handful of men whose quarrels can split it. He says that the two most capable men in the committee, Stalin and Trotsky, are a danger to its unity. Then he describes them, the one after the other. Stalin, having become General Secretary, has concentrated an enormous power in his hands, and Lenin is not sure that he always knows how to use that power with sufficient caution. Trotsky is perhaps the most capable man in the committee, but too sure of himself, and too taken with the administrative side of things. Zinoviev and Kamenev's stand in October 1917 was no accident, though it can no more be held against them personally than Trotsky's past can be held against him. Bukharin is the party's most valuable theorist, but his views can only with great doubt be called fully Marxist.

The second part is a postscript dated 4 January. Stalin, it says, is too rude. This is tolerable among us communists, and it becomes intolerable in a General Secretary. Lenin proposes that the comrades think of a way to remove Stalin from that post and put in his place a man who is more patient, more loyal, more polite and more considerate to his comrades, less capricious. A third paper, on the question of the nationalities, attacks Stalin, Ordzhonikidze and Dzerzhinsky for the "Great-Russian chauvinism" with which they have treated the Georgian communists.

It is not a programme and it is not a verdict. A man who is dying, and who knows it, is trying to arrange his party's affairs while there is still time. The names in it are the names of the men who will decide the future of the International, and the German party's fortunes will depend on them. The Zentrale has to say what is to be done with a copy of it.

The leadership meets that night in a back room. It does not take long to see how the factions would use it.

Brandler and Thalheimer, who think the party's strength lies in its workers and not in the corridors of the Comintern, would prefer to find a friend among the Russian leaders who thinks as they do. Bukharin is such a man, and so is Rykov, who chairs the Soviet government and looks after the peasants and the market. The Right's old Russian friends would be the stronger for the paper.

The Left, under Fischer and Maslow, would like to be the first to use it, and the people they would use it for are Trotsky and Radek, the ones in the Politburo who still talk of the German revolution as an urgent matter. Thälmann, who is loyal to the secretary's office because it has been loyal to him, is silent and angry. Zetkin's letter had said that a copy might be read here. It had not said what should be done afterwards.

Moscow's response will depend on the choice. The Russian leaders cannot see us decide, but they will know what we did.

- @left: Pass it to Radek and the Left Opposition in Moscow.
- @right: Pass it to Bukharin, Rykov and the defenders of the NEP.
- @publish: Print extracts in the party press.
- @hold: Keep the copy in the safe and say nothing.

